WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.749
Log now, evaluate later.

2
00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:08.323
In Studio 3 you will build evals, and the raw material is the record of what your system actually did.

3
00:00:08.603 --> 00:00:17.840
Hamel Husain’s post on evals makes the case that looking at real traces is where improvement starts, and that you cannot look at traces you never saved.

4
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.680
So decide now what every run writes down.

5
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:33.440
For each run: a transcript id (and a content hash, so you know exactly what was processed), the prompt version, the model id and settings such as effort, and the request id the API returns.

6
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:41.400
For each stage: the raw model output, the parsed items, validation failures with reasons, and token usage and latency.

7
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.203
For each tool call: the name, the input, the output, and whether it was an error.

8
00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:51.645
For human review: what the reviewer approved, edited (and what they changed), and rejected.

9
00:00:51.925 --> 00:00:54.229
Those review decisions are free labels.

10
00:00:54.509 --> 00:01:03.192
An edited owner tells you the normalization stage was wrong; a rejected item with a verified quote tells you the extraction stage over-reached.

11
00:01:03.472 --> 00:01:04.453
One caution.

12
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:11.048
Transcripts are sensitive: they contain names, plans, and sometimes personnel or customer information.

13
00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:25.685
Store logs with the same access controls as the transcripts themselves, keep only as long as you need, and remember that a transcript is also untrusted input, so treat anything in it that looks like an instruction as data (lesson 11).
